{:receipt :datalog/join-layer-decomposition
 :date "2026-08-17"
 :implementation "nbb + datalog d1b177c, synthetic relations; script kept at
                  scratchpad/join-decomp/join_decomp.cljs"

 :question
 "kotobase-peer bench/results/2026-08-14-scan-layer-decomposition.edn measured
  ONE clause, found 72% of the per-row cost is projecting into :find-ordered
  vectors and 22% is hashing them into a set, and then explicitly refused to
  generalise: `It does not measure the join's own bookkeeping across multiple
  clauses -- only the single-clause path. A three-clause join may spend its
  time differently.` The ~16x headroom argument rests entirely on that
  unanswered question. This answers it."

 :method
 "Synthetic graph so selectivity and output size are controlled rather than
  inherited: 4,000 persons, fanout 8, 40,000 quads -- knows edges plus one
  functional `name` and one functional `city` per person, so 1-, 2- and
  3-clause queries all return exactly 32,000 rows and clause count is the only
  variable. For each shape: time `datalog.core/q` whole, then time each clause
  ALONE through `datalog.query/query`. residual = total - sum(clauses), i.e.
  everything the join does beyond scanning its clauses in isolation.

  Then a SCALE CONTROL the single-clause receipt could not do: the same
  3-clause query on a quarter of the graph. If the residual is per-ROW work it
  falls with the row count; if it is per-CLAUSE planning it stays flat. Nothing
  else differs."

 :runtime-caveat
 "nbb (SCI), not the JVM. Absolutes are ~6x the JVM receipt's because SCI
  interprets -- 13,722 ns/row here against 2,105 ns/row there for the
  comparable case. The SHARE is what is being read, and the 1-clause share
  cross-checks: 74.6% here against 72% on the JVM, on a different runtime with
  different data. That agreement is what licenses reading the other rows as
  shares."

 :results
 [{:case "1-clause"  :out-rows 32000 :total-ms 588.5  :residual-share 0.746 :residual-ns-per-out-row 13722}
  {:case "2-clause"  :out-rows 32000 :total-ms 1616.2 :residual-share 0.866 :residual-ns-per-out-row 43752}
  {:case "3-clause"  :out-rows 32000 :total-ms 1835.7 :residual-share 0.895 :residual-ns-per-out-row 51360}
  {:case "3-clause, quarter graph (scale control)"
   :out-rows 8000 :total-ms 379.1 :residual-share 0.891 :residual-ns-per-out-row 42244}]

 :answer
 "The single-clause finding TRANSFERS, and it gets larger with clause count.

  1. Scanning the clauses is a small and nearly constant part of a join. At
     constant output size the sum of clause scans barely moved across shapes
     (176 -> 207 -> 217 ms) while the total grew 588 -> 1,616 -> 1,836 ms.
     Each additional join adds its own per-row cost on the SAME rows.

  2. The residual is per-ROW work, not per-clause planning. Quartering the
     graph took output rows 32,000 -> 8,000 and left residual cost per row
     essentially unchanged (51,360 -> 42,244 ns, within 18%), while total time
     fell roughly in proportion to rows. Planning overhead would have stayed
     flat in milliseconds and quadrupled per row; it did neither."

 :what-follows
 "The queries that are still behind Neo4j are the multi-clause ones -- IC02 at
  3.07x and IC09 at 7.38x -- and those sit in the 87-90% residual regime, not
  the 75% one. So the layer worth attacking is the same one the single-clause
  receipt named, and the fraction of total time it holds is larger than that
  receipt could see."

 :what-this-does-not-say
 ["The residual is everything beyond scanning clauses in isolation, which
   INCLUDES necessary join work -- probing, binding propagation, dedup. It is
   an upper bound on what stopping intermediate materialisation could recover,
   not an estimate of the recoverable amount. Separating those two needs an
   instrumented join, not a subtraction."
  "Synthetic uniform relations. Real LDBC clauses have skewed selectivity, and
   the dataset is not on this machine, so this could not be re-run against it."
  "It proposes no mechanism. Reasoning from a decomposition straight to a
   mechanism has failed twice on this codebase already -- 2026-08-02 made IC02
   worse, 2026-08-13 measured an empty answer -- and the discipline that
   finally worked was a one-line A/B read as a paired ratio."
  "Measured at host load average 35. Load-sensitive absolutes are reported only
   beside that number; the conclusions rest on within-run ratios."]}
